# Install as /etc/multirag/api.env, owner root:multirag, mode 0640.
#
# This file is the ONLY place the Channel master encryption key belongs in a
# systemd deployment. Do not copy it into the supervisor's env file, and do not
# put the key in configs/local.service_conf.yaml: every process that calls
# get_app_config() reads that YAML, including the worker children the supervisor
# forks, which would defeat the env scrubbing in _spawn_worker.
#
# Losing this key makes every stored provider credential permanently
# undecryptable. There is no re-encryption flow; back it up in your secret
# manager before first use.
#
# The value is an ordered key ring, so rotating is survivable: put the new key
# FIRST and keep the retired one behind it. The first key encrypts new
# credentials; every key on the ring can still decrypt the rows it wrote.
# Replacing the value outright is not a rotation -- it orphans every stored
# credential. A single key (the form below) is just a ring of one.
#   MULTIRAG_CHANNELS__CONTROL__SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=[<new-key>, <retired-key>]
MULTIRAG_CHANNELS__CONTROL__SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<persistent-key-from-secret-manager>
MULTIRAG_CHANNELS__CONTROL__INTERNAL_API_TOKEN=<shared-master-workload-token>

# Optional tuning (same defaults as the supervisor side):
# MULTIRAG_CHANNELS__CONTROL__SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=86400
# MULTIRAG_CHANNELS__CONTROL__DEDUPE_TTL_SECONDS=86400

# Hardening option: instead of EnvironmentFile, pass the key through systemd
# credentials (LoadCredential=) so it never lands in the unit file. That
# currently needs a small shim in ExecStart to export the credential as an
# environment variable, because the config layer reads env vars only.
